n quincy adams who'd grow up to be  the sixth president but for that to  happen america first needed to become  independent and both abigail and john  were going to have front row seats to  the coming war  [Music]  when people talk of the founding fathers  there's a tendency to paint them as  being saints dudes in fancy coats  thinking deeply and conducting  themselves with utmost decorum but for  those actually around them the founding  fathers weren't abstract icons they were  real people real people who could  sometimes be really annoying for abigail  adams that annoyance came from jon  spending barely any time at home instead  vanishing for days on into boston or  even outside massachusetts for work the  result was abigail spending vast periods  of time alone time in which she was  expected to raise their children oversee  the farm sought out the finances and  generally deal with everything it was a  grim life for a bright young woman one  made worse by jon repeatedly forgetting  to write her letters while away to be  fair tim though john adams had other  things on his mind things like the  colonies deteriorating relationship with  the british one of the reasons john was  having to move about so much was because  the stamp act attacks on every piece of  paper used had just been levied causing  his legal work to dry up while it would  be repealed in 1766 subsequent british  attempts to attack the colonists were  driving things to boiling point  everybody could feel there was a  disaster looming and on march the 5th  1770 it finally arrived the boston  massacre was the killing of five unarmed  men by british soldiers after they were  cornered by a yelling mob by this time  adams has had been living in boston  itself for two years two years in which  abigail was able to abate her loneliness  somewhat and jon was able to get into  local politics the massacre though took  him to the big time but not in the way  they might think when the trial of the  soldiers came it was john who defended  them arguing they'd been provoked by the  mob he won the case the brits were  acquitted naturally bostonians were  absolutely furious the entire family  became a flame of discontent to which a  whole bunch of angry moths were being  drawn but was abigail upset  no not one bit despite her growing  anti-british sentiment abigail fully  supported her husband's decision to take  the case not just for the publicity  boost it gave his career but for the  principles at stake the last bit is  important because abigail adams wasn't  merely a dutiful wife standing by her  man while she'd always defend her  husband vociferously she was also more  than happy to slap him down when she  thought he was wrong as  we'll soon see for now though the case  made jon's name starting him down a  twisting path that would in 1774 lead to  him being invited to the first  continental congress sadly for abigail  that would mean even more time stuck at  home alone the next few months were  basically like watching a movie about  all the epic stuff you remember from  your american history class but one  focused entirely on a character forcibly  kept away from all the epicness as the  congress formed and dissolved then  reformed again in the wake of the  battles of lexington and concord abigail  adams found herself trapped in the role  of spectator while she used this time to  develop her political thinking helped by  correspondence with early feminists like  mercy otis warren it was mostly a  baronere in her life in one letter she  called being stuck on the sidelines my  patriotic sacrifice so it was  practically a relief when the war hit  their part of massachusetts the outbreak  of fighting upended the lives of  everyone in braintree not least abigail  adams as refugees streamed in she and  her son john quincy were able to stand  on the lawl and watch the distant battle  of bunker hill with war came more  exciting work in secret letters she  informed john of british troop movements  at the massachusetts colony general  court she took up a post rooting out  female spies and women with pro-british  sympathies yet the challenge to  abigail's life in those years wouldn't  be things becoming more exciting but  that she'd soon find herself more alone  than ever  the years of the second continental  congress were hard ones for abigail  adams while jon was off making speeches  and rocking a powdered wig she was  dealing with not just loneliness and the  stress of running a farming household  but also a heap of additional disasters  there was the smallpox outbreak of 1776  which got so bad that abigail was forced  to get the children inoculated a  terrifying decision in an era when  inoculation carried a small but  significant risk of death  and there was the ever-present fear of  an armed attack in 1777 abigail lost an  unborn child while hiding from a british  army rumored to be bearing down on  boston but we don't want to give the  impression that she was just some  passive actor buffeted by the winds of  history even in the depths of her  despair abigail adams was working to  shape her nation's future most  remarkably this came in the form of her  letters to john demanding the soon-to-be  independent congress make equality one  of its central pillars abigail c neither  guys who wrote the sentence we hold  these truths to be self-evident that all  men are created equal were indeed just  focusing on the equality of men folk  hence her remember the ladies letter of  march 1776 in which she both asked john  to codify female rights and threatened  him if he didn't saying if particular  attention is not paid to the ladies we  are determined to foment a rebellion and  will not hold ourselves bound by any  laws in which we have no voice or  representation nor was she just  interested in helping white women as far  back as 1774 abigail had written to john  on the issue of slavery telling him i  wish my sincerely that there was not a  slave in the province it always seemed  to me to fight ourselves for what we are  robbing the negroes of who have as good  a right to freedom as we have for the  era she lived in this was radical stuff  suggesting an america where not just  property only white guys but also women  and maybe even non-whites could have a  stake sadly it was too radical even jon  brushed her requests off when the  declaration of independence was signed  it said nothing about women being equal  still the severing of ties with britain  did at least mean a load of shiny new  posts and duties and for john adams that  meant once again vanishing off and  leaving his wife utterly alone between  1778 and 1783 john spent most of his  time in europe on various diplomatic  missions taking their sons john quincy  and charles with him that meant yet more  empty months in which abigail was forced  to sit in the ant chamber of history  missing out on the action while her  husband  repeatedly forgot to write if you grew  up with the sanitized version of their  story you might remember hearing how  devoted to one another the atoms as were  and how much john admired abigail's  intellect while that's true it also  misses out how utterly crappy their  relationship sometimes was in this era  of loneliness abigail told people she  felt as if she were widowed she even  tried to find solace in an unconsummated  affair with a notorious ladies man  finally in 1784 she seems to have had  enough of just waiting around taking her  daughter naby with her abigail boarded a  boat for europe leaving war shattered  america behind the move likely saved a  marriage for the next four years the  adams has lived together in london now  at peace with the us finally able to  enjoy one another's company as one of  the couple's modern biographers joseph j  ellis writes of this time they learn to  love each other at an even deeper level  than before and locked that love in  place and nothing they experienced for  the rest of their lives ever threatened  their mutual trust by the time they  returned to the now independent united  states in 1788 the era of abigail being  just an observer of history  was over from now on she and her husband  would work together to shape events for  millions  the first election in american history  was probably the most predictable  running effectively unopposed george  washington became president after a  lengthy ballot process what was exciting  though was the race for vice presidents  in this era parties didn't really yet  exist with the primary divisions being  between the federalists who john adams  supported and the anti-federalists that  meant no such thing as tickets with  people voting for a vp chosen by the  presidential nominee instead the vice  presidency was dependent on the votes of  electors and in the end those electors  gave john adams a plurality the  ascension of john to the number two spot  in the government made abigail adams the  first second lady in u.s history  although the term wasn't yet used at the  time in fact a lot of things that we  associate with modern elected government  simply didn't exist the capital for one  thing was still new york a city abigail  admired and enjoyed living in  unfortunately for her it would switch to  philadelphia after just one year at a  city she hated with miserable weather  that triggered her rheumatism as a  result the second lady would spend most  of her time back on their farm in  braintree itself soon to be renamed  quincy another way governance differed  was that the vice president's family  didn't get any official help at all  while washington was given a modest home  to live in and had the resources to  throw lavish parties the addamses were  forced to find themselves a house and  pay for everything since this coincided  with abigail and john having financial  difficulties it meant living a spartan  existence on the fringes of society  continually ignored in favor of richer  members of the cabinet speakin