uincy lads do at the poor geese and  turkeys it was a bleak assessment of the  state of american politics a dark vision  of what was to come  unfortunately  is also right  abigail adams became america's second  first lady on march the 4th 1797 at the  age of 52. it was the highest profile  role that should hold in her lifetime  and that was a problem because if  there's one thing we know about abigail  adams it's that she was more than happy  to speak her mind and wars on her mind  in 1797 was the way the press and the  democratic republicans were out for a  husband after a bruising election john  adams assumed office with his enemies  determined to make him fail the press  was merciless in its treatment of him  when abigail adams responded with  withering condescension towards  jefferson's followers the press attacked  her as well at times this edged into  dark territory because she found  philadelphia so unpleasant abigail was  still spending a lot of time in  massachusetts this meant writing to john  to keep up to date but people started  intercepting their letters and  publishing their contents a sort of  low-tech version of hacking someone's  emails perhaps unsurprisingly abigail  was rather pissed she began writing her  own partisan attacks having federalist  newspapers publish anonymous screeds  kneecapping her opponents with her acid  tongue no doubt it was incredibly  cathartic yet it also did very little to  help the knives were out for the second  president and in some quarters  everything the couple did was merely  another good excuse to drive the blade  even deeper there was the controversy  over john quincy who abigail had raised  to be as bookish as bright and as  argumentative as she was traits her  husband assumed would serve the boy well  as ambassador to prussia unfortunately  jqa's posting abroad triggered cries of  nepotism among jefferson supporters then  there were the controversies like the  xyz affair now we're not going to go  into too much depth on this because  we'll probably cover it in a future john  adams video but the crib sheet version  is that america sent some diplomats to  france to try and sort out a brewing  spat only for those diplomats to be  shaken down for a bribe by three men  working for the delightfully corrupt  foreign minister talarand the scandal  was so great that many pushed president  adams to declare war on france when he  stopped short of formally announcing a  conflict anti-federalist partisans  declared abigail was influencing his  judgment to be honest in many ways she  was today historians the gabigal adams  had a big hand in some of her husband's  picks for important roles and helped  nudge him on policy it's not for nothing  that the opposition's nickname of mrs  president stuck she really was a key  player but not in the xyz affair in this  instance abigail stood against osborne  demanding john declare war on paris when  it came to the most notorious aspect of  his presidency of all though abigail  would be firmly on john's side by 1798  the country was so divided and the  undeclared quasi war with france so  damaging that the first couple were  living in fear john of mass civil  disobedience abigail of violence  committed against their family hence the  alien earned seditions act an act  abigail championed but one that had gone  down in infamy a vicious assault on  civil liberties the act was  anti-immigrant pro-executive power and  extremely anti-free speech the alien's  part gave the president the power to  deport any foreigner he personally  didn't like a serious cudgel in a nation  of immigrants but the sedition's part  was even worse intended to stop partisan  attacks the act basically banned free  speech and freedom of the press  mandating arrest for anyone who insulted  the president and adams used these  powers leading democratic republican  newspapers were shut down and people  were put on trial including a  congressman who merely accused the  president of being pompous from  abigail's perspective the act was  exactly what the nation needed a firm  way for her husband to punish those who  wronged him but the crackdown would have  consequences neither of them could have  foreseen now newly energized the  democratic republicans were determined  to make sure john adams broke another  historical record the first one-term  president in history  november 1800 saw the adams at last  leave hated philadelphia behind and  relocate to the new capital of  washington dc unfortunately the white  house wasn't finished yet building work  was ongoing and there are cute anecdotes  of abigail hanging the families washing  in the incomplete east room but while it  might have been better than philly  abigail and john's time in the federal  capital would be overshadowed by misery  the first dark cloud settled over the  white house on the 30th of november that  day abigail's son charles died of  alcoholism aged only 30. as you might  imagine this was heartbreaking not just  because they'd lost a son but because  they'd lost him in such a senseless  self-destructive way yet there was  already another dark cloud already  looming up to join the first one which  would soon soak the first lady in a bit  of rain held from october 31 to december  the 3rd the 1800 election was basically  a re-run of 1796 pitting john adams and  the federalists against jefferson's  democratic republicans it even ended  with a similar score with the winning  side just edging to victory with 73  electoral votes the difference is this  time those all-important extra votes  went to thomas jefferson from a  historical perspective the election  marked the beginning of the greatest  winning streak in u.s history the  democratic republicans would now win  every presidential election until they  imploded after 1824 a seven victory run  unmatched by any party since from  abigail adams's perspective though it  marked nothing more than the end of her  husband's career the couple left the  white house in march 1801 and returned  to quincy massachusetts under a cloud of  depression but while it sucked to lose  abigail was clear-eyed about what it  meant a few regrets she wrote to her son  at my age and with my bodily infirmities  i shall be happier at quincy in all  likelihood she was right back in  massachusetts abigail found herself  freed from the partisan attacks from the  relentless politicking and ill feelings  she found time to write striking up a  number of correspondences remarkably  this included thomas jefferson after his  daughter died in 1804 abigail wrote to  the president to offer her condolences  re-establishing contact between the two  of them although she'd always maintain  that his vision for america was the  incorrect one she would come around to  seeing the man once again as a friend of  course the big thing happening around  this time was her son's rise just as  john adams's star was fading john  quincy's was beginning to burn with  nuclear fire after switching allegiance  to the democratic republican party jqa  became ambassador to russia before  settling in britain and marrying an  english woman throughout abigail wrote  to him as she had once to john offering  advice pressing her political  convictions helping guide and shape his  path by 1818 the boy was back living in  the u.s and in the exalted position of  secretary of state for abigail this  meant a chance to bask in the glow of  her son's success to be proud of the  up-and-coming politician that she'd  helped create yet she'd never get to see  him become president on october 28 1818  abigail adams passed away at home from  typhoid fever she was 73. one of the  last acts had been to write a will  defiantly bucking the laws at the time  that said all of her money and property  belonged to the man she was married to  instead she left nearly everything to  her female relatives if congress  wouldn't dane to remember the ladies  then  by god abigail would today abigail adams  remains a central figure in the early  story of america a woman so important  that she's sometimes referred to as a  founding mother in a way that's a little  surprising as vital as she was to jon  and john quincy's political development  her most passionately held beliefs  failed to make their mark congress  forgot the ladies and slavery wouldn't  be abolished until decades after she  died but perhaps what's important is  that she pushed these ideas regardless  that she fought to make the new nation  the best version of itself that it could  possibly be she might not have succeeded  but by trying she set a powerful example  one that would inspire not just future  americans but people all over the world  [Music]  you
 

